# SYNOPSIS

```usage
notices
```

# DESCRIPTION

`notices` shows what apps tell the user, as slips under the top edge of the screen left of deskbar(4)'s panel, and keeps the drawer: a window with the history of the clipboard and of the notices.

A service whose manifest says `use notices` posts a notice by writing a line to `/svc/notices/post`, in ndb(6)'s form: `title=` (required, 80 bytes at most), `body=` (400 at most) and `lamp=`, one of `info`, `done`, `warn` and `fail` (`info` if none).
Each client has its own tree, so a notice names the service that posted it, which no client can change.
A line that breaks these rules fails its write with `invalid`.
Nothing is asked of the user, and a notice can't be answered: an app that wants an answer opens a window.

A slip shows the notice's lamp in the theme's role for it (`selection`, `live`, `warning` and `alert`), the app, the title and the body, the newest on top.
At most three show at once.
A slip of `info` or `done` goes after 6 seconds; one of `warn` or `fail` stays until it is clicked, and a click dismisses any slip.
Past three, the oldest slip that would go anyway goes now; if all three stay, the new one waits for room, and its 6 seconds start when it shows.
More than 10 notices from one app in 10 seconds are kept in the history but not shown.
The slips' panel is open only while there are slips, and their end is the only deadline `notices` waits for, so it never wakes on an empty desk.
The history is the last 100 notices, kept in memory.

`notices` reads radharc(4)'s `clipboard` (its manifest's `grant windows clipboard`), which gives each entry committed that isn't secret, and keeps each in `/data/Clips` as a file holding the entry as `clip/new` takes it, named by its number, with `Clip:Source` (the app that wrote it), `Clip:Origin` (its window's title), `Clip:Time` (seconds since the epoch, UTC) and `Clip:Types` (its types, by `,`).
An entry equal to the newest clip isn't kept again.
Clips past the limits go, the oldest first: 50 clips, 30 days and 64 MiB in all, unless Settings' `Drawer` area (settings(2)) gives `Drawer:Clips`, `Drawer:Days` or `Drawer:MiB`, read when `notices` starts.
Without a volume, the clips are kept in memory, within the same limits, until `notices` stops.

The drawer lists the clips, newest first, by their text's first line or their types, with their source and origin, and the notices below them.
Clicking a clip puts it on the clipboard again, as a new entry written to `clip/new`; Forget removes one, and Forget all every one.
Deskbar's menu has Drawer, which opens and closes it.

Its manifest:

```text
service notices
program notices
export per-client
use windows
grant windows clipboard
mount /data fs/volume optional
mount /lib/fonts bootfs/lib/fonts
restart on-failure
```

# EXAMPLES

Say a build is done, from rc(1) in a service that uses notices:

```rc
echo 'title=Build body=''amd64 in 156 s'' lamp=done' >/svc/notices/post
```

Keep ten clips at most, from the next start:

```rc
attr /data/Settings/Drawer Drawer:Clips 10
```

# FILES

: `/svc/notices/post`
  Write only: a notice a line.
: `/svc/notices/ctl`
  Write only: `drawer` opens the drawer, or closes it; `dismiss <id>` dismisses a slip; `paste <n>` puts clip `n` on the clipboard again; `forget <n>` removes clip `n`; `clear` removes every clip.
  A verb that names nothing there fails with `invalid`.
: `/svc/notices/status`
  Read only, a line each: `ready`; `slips <n>`; `slip <id> <app> <lamp> <title>` for each slip shown, newest first; `waiting <n>`; `history <n>`; `clips <n>`; `clip <n> <source> <types>` for each clip, oldest first; `drawer open` or `closed`; `clipboard open` or `none`, whether it reads Radharc's; and `wakeups <n>`, how many times its loop has woken.
: `/data/Clips`
  The clips, a file each.

